  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so plain steps with a learning rate above 0.5 overshoot more each time along x and the run diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian and centred on zero, with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. Because of the noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at their true length in bowl units, using one shared scale. Orange shows the step the ball will take next, against the noisy slope. Grey shows the step it would take using the true full-data slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the ball leaves the drawn bowl or the loss goes above 10 (diverged). A diverged ball is drawn at the bowl's edge, but the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed plus the run number. Reset replays the same noise. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next noise values in the sequence. While running, the ball takes five steps a second."
  ]
}